  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "summary#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "Summary",
      "excerpt": "An org's offices and events each get a public space and a private space. Each of",
      "text": "Make sure this list can be expanded later, and can be changed \"per organization\"",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T18:05:19.869Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "two roles",
      "offset": 176,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T18:05:40.271Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T18:16:32.922Z",
          "html": "<p>Done. Lead and Member are where an org starts: each org can rename them and add its own. The protocol only cares who's on a Space's list, so none of this touches it.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T18:14:52.526Z"
      }
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "summary#message-1",
      "kind": "message",
      "label": "Summary",
      "excerpt": "Nobody can write while Spell's server is down, and it holds the right to write f",
      "text": "I'm imagining that the \"spell server\" in question here is just gatekeeping writes for now and maybe writing.  Gatekeeping reads is deferred but should be possible trivially.\n\nNote that privacy-focused orgs will want to host their own atmosphere server (DID? DSP? can't remember the name), and that's fine.  \n\nWe may require that in the first version for public spaces.  We can trial this by creating spaces/etc for me personally and generalize for this.\n\n\"Setting up an org meta-space\" using one of the existing hosts needs to be dead easy, requiring no knowledge of atprotocol.  \n\nSetting up a \"private org meta-space\" (running the DPD or whatever) needs to be simple, but may require knowlegge of ATProto",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T18:06:24.342Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Nobody can write while Spell's server is down",
      "offset": 0,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T18:10:49.215Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T18:16:34.006Z",
          "html": "<p>Worked in: the first version's server guards writes only, in \"How it maps to the protocol\", and there's a new section, \"Setting up an org\", with both paths. It's a PDS you were thinking of, a Personal Data Server.</p>\n<p>Two things follow. It has to do the writing too, because only the holder of the org's right can write to its repo. And with no read guard, public pages are readable by anyone with the address, while a private space's Leads need accounts.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T18:14:53.689Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "addresses",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "Addresses and messages",
      "excerpt": "Addresses and messages",
      "text": "You are right that notification to device is killer feature of discord, add this to the plan.   Must work on ios + android.\n\nCould be that people install the spell app on their phone for this.\n\nA \"master app per org,\" branded and customized for that org, should be possible after initial release.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T18:11:15.914Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "email",
      "offset": 401,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T18:13:25.684Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T18:16:34.978Z",
          "html": "<p>Added: a new section, \"Alerts on phones\", and a step in the plan, for iOS and Android. It starts with an installed web app, tried early on real phones, with store apps if that falls short. The branded app per org comes after the first release, once Apple's limits on template apps are checked.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T18:14:54.679Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "spaces",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "Spaces and roles",
      "excerpt": "Spaces and roles",
      "text": "Someone leaving a role or an org doesn't mean that the data they added goes away -- it just means that they can't write anymore and can't see private spaces.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T18:15:17.974Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Four more rules",
      "offset": 805,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T18:15:52.938Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-01T18:19:24.145Z",
          "html": "<p>Added as a rule: someone leaving a role or an org can't write any more and can't see private spaces, and what they added stays.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-01T18:19:10.545Z"
      }
    }
  },
